    "case_id": "706",
    "case_summary": "A 34-year-old woman with a history of severe obesity and unipolar major depression with psychotic features, on lurasidone (92.5mg/day) for four months. The evening prior to symptom onset, the patient completed a 5 km flat walk, which was unusual for her otherwise sedentary routine. Three days later, she presented to the emergency department with bilateral foot drop and painful swelling of the legs.\n\nPatient presented with:- Bilateral peroneal nerve paralysis- Hardened painful forelegs- Visibly swollen and reddish colored legs- Temperature: 37.9°C\n\nLaboratory Tests:- CRP: 280mg/dL- WBC: 25,000/μL- Neutrophils: 20,000/μL- CPK: 15,000U/L- GOT: 409U/L- GPT: 152U/L- LDH: 1322U/LImaging Studies:1. Emergency Echographic Assessment:- Showed indirect signs of myositis and panniculitis- Main arteries and veins of both lower limbs were patent2. Electroneurography (ENG):- Showed bilateral motor nerve block below the fibular head3. Electromyography (EMG):- No signs of spontaneous nor voluntary activity in peroneal innervated muscles4. MRI Findings:Image Title: Magnetic resonance imaging of leg musclesImage Description: - Rounded morphology of both peroneal muscle compartments- Outward convex bulging of the overlying crural fascia- Muscle edema- Area of necrosis in both tibialis anterior muscles",
